.TH WINDOWBORDER-NATIVE 1 "2026-10-05" "kwin-windowborder 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
windowborder-native \- give applications the native KWin/Breeze window border
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B windowborder-native
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Gives selected applications the window border that KWin draws itself, for
windows that have no visible boundary of their own: Tauri/WebView and other
applications that draw their title bar themselves, or that ask for
\f(CWdecorations: false\fR.
.PP
Two native settings are written:
.IP \(bu 2
a window rule in \f(CW~/.config/kwinrulesrc\fR that forces "No titlebar and
frame = false" (\f(CWnoborder=false\fR with \f(CWnoborderrule=2\fR). This makes
KWin create a server-side decoration for the window;
.IP \(bu 2
a per-window override in \f(CW~/.config/breezerc\fR (\f(CW[Windeco Exception
N]\fR) with \f(CWHideTitleBar=true\fR and \f(CWBorderSize=None\fR: no title bar
is drawn, the border width stays zero so the window geometry does not change,
and the 1px border outline remains as the visible border. The left, right and
bottom edges stay usable for mouse resizing.
.PP
Both files are rewritten from one application list, and KWin is asked to
re-read them afterwards. This command is also the backend of the KWin script
extension and of \fBwindowborder-daemon\fR(1); the window menu and the settings
panel call it rather than reimplementing it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B add \fIapp\fR...
Add the given window classes (the Wayland \f(CWapp_id\fR, i.e. what KWin
matches as \f(CWresourceClass\fR) and apply immediately.
.TP
.B remove \fIapp\fR...
Remove the given window classes and apply immediately.
.TP
.B set \fIkey value\fR
Change an appearance setting:
\f(CWBorderSize\fR (\f(CWNone\fR, \f(CWNoSides\fR, \f(CWTiny\fR,
\f(CWNormal\fR, \f(CWLarge\fR, ...) or \f(CWHideTitleBar\fR
(\f(CWtrue\fR/\f(CWfalse\fR). \f(CWBorderSize=None\fR is the default and the only
one that leaves the window geometry untouched.
.TP
.B apply
Regenerate the rules from the current configuration and apply them, without
changing the configuration. Useful after editing
\f(CW~/.config/windowborder-native.conf\fR by hand, or when the rules were lost
(for example after a KWin upgrade).
.TP
.B status
Print the configuration, the rules in \f(CWkwinrulesrc\fR and the per-window
overrides in \f(CWbreezerc\fR.
.TP
.B reset
Remove every rule and override this tool manages, and delete
\f(CW~/.config/windowborder-native.conf\fR.
.TP
.B request \fIrequest\fR
Internal entry point used by \fBwindowborder-daemon\fR(1):
\f(CWadd:\fIapp\fR, \f(CWremove:\fIapp\fR, \f(CWpanel\fR, \f(CWreapply\fR,
\f(CWlist\fR or \f(CWreset\fR.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/windowborder-native.conf
The application list and the appearance settings (the canonical state).
.TP
.I ~/.config/kwinrulesrc
KWin window rules; one rule per application.
.TP
.I ~/.config/breezerc
Breeze window-specific overrides; one \f(CW[Windeco Exception N]\fR group per
application.
.TP
.I ~/.config/kwinrc
The \f(CW[Script-windowborder-menu]\fR group mirrors the application list for
the settings panel of the KWin script extension.
.SH NOTES
\f(CWdecorationPolicy\fR is one-way: adding the rule switches a window to
server-side decoration, removing it does not switch it back, so a window only
returns to its own decoration after the application is restarted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR windowborder-daemon (1),
.BR kwin_wayland (1)
